WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Adventures of Bayou Billy, you're a raging Cajun on the tail of a swamp rat.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:28.380
And welcome to NEStalgia, chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.260
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.440
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.550
This game seems to be leaning into the 1986 film Crocodile Dundee.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.160
You know, just a move.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.060
I'm not talking about the movie itself, but just a move.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.740
Strategically, marketing, Konami, Nintendo.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.600
I just can't figure out, like, why would you target Crocodile Dundee like that?

00:00:52.600 --> 00:01:01.900
I mean, okay, I guess like, yeah, the hat and the knife, but everything else is way too New Orleans based for me to make that assessment.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.480
Yeah. They're like in the city at some point in this game, right?

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:12.740
Yeah. And Crocodile Dundee is like, that's just Australia, which is probably the farthest from New Orleans I can think.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.900
But there's something about like even just the cover of the game and the poster for the movie.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.180
If you put them side by side, it feels like somebody plagiarized.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.880
Yeah. It's like they just, maybe they like airlifted them out of Australia and into New Orleans.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.320
Right, right. So I'm not saying like, I agree.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.320
Like, yeah, they are two separate plots and everything like that.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:44.460
But I don't know, like, why would you be like, you know, we should, we should seek inspiration from Crocodile Dundee for our Japanese video game set in New Orleans.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.380
It's a fun adventure, right?

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.700
I never saw Crocodile Dundee.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.320
Yeah, I think that that's pretty much all that you can say about its aesthetic is just fun adventure.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.980
And it is a fun adventure, right?

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:59.160
I mean, you play as Billy West, otherwise known as Bayou Billy.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.860
They alternated on that in the manual.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:09.820
I can't tell if like Bayou Billy is like a known nickname for him or if they just like, well, we put that on the title of the game.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.160
So we should also call him Bayou Billy at some point in the manual.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:20.800
So it's clear. They also do this thing where they make it seem like there are like actors playing these characters, especially in the end credits.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.660
Like they gave each character like a real name.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.820
Well, that's a Konami thing, right?

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.820
Yeah, we've had games like that before.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.040
Yeah, they seem to do that a lot where it's like, oh, it was all a movie.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.240
Like nobody really got hurt.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.580
It was all a movie.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.400
They pulled that twist on us in Castlevania.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.660
Yeah, that's right. You're out about that.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:49.580
And then, okay, so it's Bayou Billy and then also Godfather Gordon, the local crime boss who kidnaps Billy's girlfriend, Annabelle.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.160
And, you know, that's really all you need to know about the plot.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.820
But what does that say about like how close that is to – and I'm not saying this game is like Super Mario Brothers.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:06.240
But, you know, how close it is to Super Mario Brothers that like – is that just like a known video game thing at this time that like it's just simple enough?

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.500
If you don't have a plot, just default to the villain capturing the girlfriend or the princess or something.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.940
I think that's just a storytelling thing in general.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.880
It doesn't matter if it's a video game or an action movie.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.500
I think this is one of just those boilerplate plots that you can slap on anything.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.260
Would you argue that that's still true though, Sean?

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:38.420
You know, whereas like now it seems like when movies do that trope, it's taken to almost like parody levels of, you know – not that Taken is a parody.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:47.480
But, you know, like that's a hyper-extreme movie where like, you know, they're clearly going off of the trope of like, you know, his daughter has been taken and now he's going to stop at nothing.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.200
And he – you know, the action scenes are over the top.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.480
I think that you just answered your question.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.400
I think that – I mean, yeah, Taken is one of the most basic versions of this.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:13.780
But I think if you expanded the scope of this to like, oh, it's just a kidnapping plus this and that, then I think that a lot more movies and TV shows would probably fall into it.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:22.440
I don't know. I also think like particularly with early video games right now, like think of how many times we've had this trope just on this podcast so far.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:30.420
And I think back then, you know, not always, but a lot of the time people were – you know, developers were not like we need to make the best story in this video game.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:38.500
Story was just one little checkmark in a list of things they were trying to make with like gameplay and graphics probably being more important to a lot of these developers.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.200
So they probably just picked the most generic like, oh, yeah, there's a story.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.040
Okay. Well, maybe we can make a fun adventure out of this classic thing.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:05:02.000
Yeah. And while you move through the game through like, you know, different places and locations and even different genres of gameplay, it just seems like at the end of every level, it's just a reminder that Godfather Gordon has Annabelle.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.520
And I don't even know if we like really needed that.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.520
Like also, how is he communicating with Bayou Billy between stages?

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:08.880
Does he call him on a phone?

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.200
No, no, no. It's just a scene.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.200
It's a scene change.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.460
At least the audience knows.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.880
But he's speaking directly to Billy.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.200
Like, oh, you got through that.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.500
You know?

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:33.800
Stepping away for a second from like the story itself, like I – sorry, but like this manual is probably my favorite of all the ones that we've read so far.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:47.640
Because in addition to this ridiculous story that is really just, you know, different decorations on the same archetypical plot that we keep seeing, it's colorfully written.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.660
It's not – there's no like huge grammatical errors.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.660
It seems to be having fun.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.180
So I just wanted to point that out.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.040
Yeah, I chuckled a few times while reading that.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.440
It's funny. They're pouring a lot of love into it for sure.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:12.940
But I guess that's what I'm saying is it's like it seems to be that like the plot is or, you know, at least the characters they've designed have a lot more like – they have more of a vision for it.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.980
But then the actual game like never does anything with it.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.800
I guess like when you look at like Ninja Gaiden, right, you could say that that's kind of similar, right?

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.720
Like when you boil it down, it is the same trope.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:28.800
But like there's actual like plot turns and like, you know, oh, like your father's dead now.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:33.780
And like, no, it's turned out that he's been turned into this evil enemy that, you know, is revealed to you.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.180
It's like those things all sound great.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:42.040
And it seems like Konami has a vision of that but then just decided like but we don't really need to like show that in the game.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.020
Like we'll just keep reminding you that Annabelle is captured.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.980
I don't see Bowser telling you like at the end of every level like, hey, just a reminder.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.820
I'm really close to beating the shit out of Peach.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:05.760
But also going back to kind of what you were saying before, not using the taken example but just the fact that this trope now in movies is a lot of times used in parody as well.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:11.220
Like in like where things are over the top ridiculous because it's like, oh, this trope's been done a million times.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.100
I almost feel like that's what they're doing here.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:20.460
They're like they're making it over the top because they're like, ah, like another, you know, another like guy kidnapped, you know, bad guy kidnaps your girlfriend.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:21.500
Like you got to save her.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:28.580
And they have this like, I don't know, this goofy story that revolves around it that kind of pokes fun at itself.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.580
Yeah. What's the Keanu movie with the dogs where the dog, you know, his dog is kidnapped?

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.500
John Wick. Oh, yeah.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.780
Like where is the John Wick video game?

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:42.060
Like the hyper, like, you know, Bowser steals Peach again.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:49.080
And this time Mario's taking everything like to the next level of like stylization and, you know, just stunting on everybody.

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.800
Like a Shadow the Hedgehog but in the Mario world.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.240
You know what? There was a Shadow the Hedgehog game and maybe that is the plot.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.760
Maybe somebody took like he had a scientist he loved in Sonic Adventure 2.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.640
Maybe they took her and Marie was her name.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:08.100
I would love to see a, like a misanthropic emo Mario.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:09.440
I think that would be pretty cool.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:11.740
People always wanted an M-rated Mario.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:12.640
I don't understand why.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.920
I don't seem to feel there at all.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.040
Maybe, you know, they're like, well, we grew up with Mario so Mario should grow up with us.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.900
I want a Mario with a face tattoo.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:23.480
Yeah. I want a sleeping dogs Mario.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:33.540
Yeah, I agree. Open world GTA-like but, you know, set in that feudal Japan Super Mario Odyssey level that's all Bowser's Kingdom.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.040
What show is this again?

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:37.960
Yeah, we're getting close to talking about the game.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:39.600
Bye, you, Billy.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:48.780
Notable more so than anything for the fact that it is one-third beat-em-up, one-third light gun, one-third racing game.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.680
And racing, I think, is like a weird term.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:54.000
I took that from the wiki, but I wouldn't describe these as racing levels.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.940
They're like Mach Rider-esque.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:09:00.980
Where did we fall on like what you would call these kinds of like stages, you know?

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.400
I mean, they're driving levels.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:09.860
I wouldn't go so far to say it's a race because, I mean, I guess it's a race against time, but it's just a driving level with a timer.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.260
But is it even, is there a timer in this?

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.860
Yeah, I mean, like it's, the timer is your fuel, but it acts as a timer.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:22.560
Oh, right, right. And what do you think about like, you know, we've been praising games incorporating two genres.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.500
This is now like, hey, you guys love that shit.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.460
It seems like Konami served this up on a platter.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.900
They're like, oh, I heard you guys like games inside games.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.160
So here's three.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:34.680
You know, I appreciated it.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:41.920
I get a vibe that maybe I won't be fully agreed with, but I think that they did a great job of making a well-rounded game.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:52.800
While that might have meant sometimes it was like a jack-of-all-trades, master of none, I thought it was a good, you know, every time like a change in play style came, it was welcome.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:56.280
Because it was like right when I was like probably would have started to get bored with the last one.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:58.900
And it kept it fresh for me.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:05.760
And I thought that they were each at least passable, but at best, okay, pretty good in some cases.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:06.920
Wow. All right.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.760
I guess you're going to have to start explaining yourself in these more in-depth sections now.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.280
Yeah. Go ahead.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.360
Before we get it too in-depth, though, I would say one thing, Joe.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:25.560
You know, if you're saying that like it's great that they switched it up more often and gave you the variety in it, like things didn't get too stale, right?

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:37.560
I'd say you had me until you said things didn't get too stale because I would argue that these levels, which seem short if you look at them on like a strategy wiki website or something like that, right?

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.500
If you look at like the maps, you're like, oh, it's not that long.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.080
But the actual levels themselves, I felt like all overstayed their welcome no matter which one it was.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:49.920
If it was the beat-em-up stages, I was like, okay, don't really need like another round of fighters here.

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:51.760
Like I feel like these fights go on forever.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:55.280
If it's the light gun thing, I'm like, you know, I'm getting shot at a lot.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.600
It's going to be hard to survive.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:01.260
If it's the driving levels, I'm like, wow, that's a really long way to the finish.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:04.200
Like it's going to take me a while to drive through all of this.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:07.580
So they just seem like I would cycle through them more often.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.160
See, I – well, I mean I disagree with what you just said with everything except for the beat-em-up stages.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.760
I completely was like by the end of every beat-em-up stage like just get me out of this stage because I do think –

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.220
but I think that was more because of the mechanics of the beat-em-up stage were the ones that weren't perfect.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:26.180
Or like I think it was not even necessarily that the stage itself was –

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:32.900
I think the reason that the stage was too long was because they just made the enemies like take too long to kill.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.220
So like they made each little thing you have to do take too long.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:38.580
And I thought that that got a little annoying.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:46.100
But as far as the driving stage and the – especially the light gun stage, I thought that those were engaging straight through for me.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:50.600
I mean I never thought that – first of all, I thought the light gun stages were kind of short.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.240
Like I could have gone for a little longer on those.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.940
And I didn't use a light gun.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:01.340
I used the controller, which surprisingly worked really well, which I wouldn't have thought would happen

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:04.240
because I don't think I've played one of these light gun games without a light gun yet.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:10.940
Yeah, you weren't on our Operation Wolf episode last week where that was also one that you could use a controller on.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:16.820
But yeah, so I guess we have a little bit of a difference of opinion of those other two stages.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:18.960
But I thought those other two stages were really well balanced.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:35.980
So let's break it down then.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:41.600
Let's go into the beat-em-up first, something that very closely mirrors the Double Dragon style of gameplay

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.760
that is currently prevalent in NES beat-em-up games.

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:48.220
I mentioned Double Dragon because that's our reference for how to do it right.

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.900
That's on our Essential Games list.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.320
I think it's the only beat-em-up on our Essential Games list.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:55.740
So it's taking that Double Dragon style.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:13:01.360
It's giving you a button for punching, a button for kicking, and then push both of them together,

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:04.300
and you get a jump kick, which sounds great on paper,

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:08.820
but I didn't appreciate that the only way to jump is to also do the jump kick.

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:11.800
There should have just been the up arrow on the D-pad, which isn't preferred.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:15.980
But there just should have been another way to do jumping in this game because that just feels weird.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:21.680
If you need to jump in this game and there's no one to attack, you still have to do the jump attack button.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.420
But that's a small nitpick.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:25.120
Sean, thoughts on the beat-em-up?

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:32.700
I had high hopes for the beat-em-up sections until I actually started fighting the guys.

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:38.260
And like Mike was saying, they take forever to kill, especially if you're hand-to-hand.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:47.540
And it just seems that whenever you push an input, there's ever so slightly a delay

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:52.500
that doesn't really occur in these other beat-em-ups that we played.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.840
And they don't seem to have that same kind of delay.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:14:00.700
They seem to just know that you're in range and immediately do a frame-perfect punch.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:04.680
You don't have enough range on your attacks.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:06.500
It all added up.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:10.560
Like, all of these things are kind of small when taken alone.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:15.960
But when they all get thrown in together, it becomes a very frustrating experience.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:24.800
And to be honest, I played through that first level five or six times until I was just like,

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:26.820
OK, I'm going to go play the practice stage.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:30.420
And OK, I'll play the practice stage for the other different modes.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:37.720
And I was like, OK, if I'm going to play through this for real, or at least not for real,

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.940
but at least experience the game, I have to cheat.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:50.400
So I had to cheat in this game just to get past the first level because the beat-em-up section is so poorly designed.

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:53.580
Sean, may I ask real fast before we go over to Joe?

00:14:54.040 --> 00:15:00.220
Was it the, like, you know, you were saying, just the normal guys that are basically acting like punching bags.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:05.000
Were you stopped by them, or did it get to the section where you have to fight the alligators?

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:10.420
I actually got through the alligators pretty easily because I think I accidentally cheesed them.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.100
Like, that I didn't have to cheat for.

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:18.360
I was just like, oh, if I stand here, they keep, like, spawning in a place that they just can't hit my hitbox.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.040
So the alligators were fine.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:30.480
It was the, like, gas mask dudes in the swamp later, along with, like, when they were paired up with the big burly dudes.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:39.980
So you've got, like, gas mask dude with a, um, uh, with a bulletproof vest, and you've got big burly dude with a rock.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:44.420
And that combination, uh, was very, very difficult.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.100
So I was just like, I can't do it anymore.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:50.020
I might be remembering this wrong, but the gas mask guys are, like, scuba guys, right?

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:51.020
Like, they're coming out from the water.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:52.720
Yeah, I guess they're more scuba.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:57.760
I just didn't want them to think, like, New Orleans had been in, like, a post-apocalyptic setting.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:15:58.920
I mean, you know.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:02.860
For context, in the manual, their name is Jacques Kilsto.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.100
Yeah. Just this game has a sense of humor.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.240
Yes, yes, you're right.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:08.200
I forgot about that.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:10.740
So, yeah, scuba, not gas mask.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.160
Strike it from the record, Mike.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:13.860
Yeah, you got it. You got it.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.580
Joe, thoughts on the beat-em-up stages?

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.060
Yeah, I mean, I definitely largely agree.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:22.120
As far as, I think the beat-em-up stages are the weakest portion of this game by far.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:28.260
And it is, I don't know that I noticed necessarily that the controls themselves were a problem.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:33.740
But it's more like the enemies, I don't know, it just feels like in any other video game,

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:39.400
it would take, like, one-sixteenth the amount of hits on an enemy to kill the enemy.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:42.120
So, like, it never ends up being like, oh, this is interesting.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:43.060
I have to think about a way to do it.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.240
It's just like, I just got to hit that guy, like, 16 times.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.400
And, like, it gets a little frustrating after a while.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:52.900
It doesn't feel like there was a lot of thought put into, like, how that would work.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:57.300
And I definitely was very annoyed with the crocodiles.

00:16:57.940 --> 00:17:02.680
And I did, like you did, Sean, I cheesed them after a while where I was able to stand a certain distance away from them

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.260
and, like, hit them at a point where they couldn't hit me.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:08.800
But, again, it's like 15 hits that I have to get on them.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:12.660
And, like, sometimes you just absolutely can't get past them.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:16.800
So, it just becomes you standing there off to the corner as they come at you.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:18.400
And there's just, like, nothing you can do but try and hit them.

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:23.960
And when you do it, when you do the cheesing way that I was doing, you only hit them one out of every, like, five swings.

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:28.240
So, it was really just, like, those really just stopped down the level for me.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:35.620
But I will say that there were some points where, like, at one point I realized, I didn't even notice, that I had a gun.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:36.560
And I picked it up from an enemy.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:37.960
I didn't even notice that, like, that was a pickup.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:39.880
I didn't even realize I had a gun.

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:45.440
Yeah. And when I started using that, like, and it would, like, kill an enemy in one hit.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:51.380
And it suddenly, I guess, you know, did a good job of making me feel like my ammo, first of all, was really valuable.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.320
Made me really think about, like, what weapon, when to use my gun, when to attack.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:18:00.160
And kind of, in that sense, like, I think it did an okay job of doing that.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.240
And especially with some of the other weapons you pick up later.

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:03.680
There was a whip that I used a lot.

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:06.940
And, you know, like, a throwing knife. Well, a whip is indispensable.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:08.480
Yeah. What's that?

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:11.280
The whip is, like, you have to use that.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:17.000
Yeah, yeah. I think unless you have the gun and you have enough ammo for it, the whip is the next best thing.

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:18.580
But it, like, keeps the enemies away from you.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:20.700
So, like, on that level, I thought it was good.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.140
But I did feel like that's the one where, you know, like I said earlier, I agree with Mike.

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:27.440
Like, that those levels are too long.

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:36.720
Well, you know, they're probably just too long, though, because of this, you know, the amount of time it takes to defeat an enemy, right?

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:40.680
Now put in a few enemies in each wave and then the number of waves.

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:43.020
I just feel like you have to make a decision.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:52.280
It's either, like, okay, there's going to be, like, you know, the enemies are really hard fights that you have to be very strategic about, like, you know, make sure, like, you know, this is a fist fight, right?

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:56.880
So they're going for some kind of realism here where, like, a bullet means an automatic death.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:01.320
But a fist fight, you know, most people don't die in, like, you know, four punches.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:06.920
Like, you're going to have, you are seriously knocking these guys unconscious because you have to hit them, like, 16 times.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:09.820
So you are really beating them down to a pulp.

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:16.840
But the tradeoff there is that now you're saying, all right, so that's the amount of hits that it's going to take because this is a fist fight and we want it to feel like that.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:25.020
However, you're really testing the limits of the player here because you can keep saying, like, okay, well, now, you know, you saw how it was with one guy.

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:26.220
All right, now it's two guys.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:27.600
Now it's three guys.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:28.980
Now it's alligators.

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:42.300
You know, it's like I feel like there was a certain decision made here where they needed to actually step back and say, like, as we're building out the waves, we should test the game at this point and see if, like, how this feels, you know?

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.060
And I'm not saying they didn't. We have no way of knowing.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:51.100
But it feels like they made the whole level, then tested it and said, well, we just programmed all this.

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.060
So we're going to keep it.

00:19:54.060 --> 00:20:05.300
But I don't know that, like, you know, I don't know if, like, the bullet, you know, and having limited ammo, like, yes, that is, like, something that is really cool feeling and, like, yes, you should conserve that stuff.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:13.440
But I don't know if it should be praised because it's, like, I don't know, just make, like, just make combat in general more interesting.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:15.220
Don't rely on, like, picking up a gun.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.040
Like, you know, you guys are both talking about the whip.

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:21.840
I'm like, I don't know if you, you know, like, shouldn't you just have always had a weapon?

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:23.160
You were coming into a fight.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.560
Like, I don't know.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:26.080
Like, should that have just been the thing?

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.900
Should you have been a little more powerful than your adversaries?

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:33.740
You're going up against a bunch of them if they're the grunts and you're no more powerful than the grunts.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.940
Well, okay, this might be a little bit of a silly comparison.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:45.560
But when I was talking about the gun thing, like, I almost think of that, like, in a slightly similar way to, like, the way The Last of Us makes me feel.

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:48.360
Where it's, like, it makes me feel about conserving my ammo.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:50.800
And, like, it makes me feel like, what do I want to use this bullet on?

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:54.120
And, yeah, it is, like, I agree with you on everything else you're saying.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:21:02.960
I definitely think that, like, it was a gameplay, like, the gameplay in these levels was kind of designed in a way that doesn't really let you, like, play it.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:04.900
You know, it makes you want to cheese it more.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:08.520
It makes you want to just, you know, avoid everyone, which you can't half the time.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:13.140
So you just got to, you know, like, I found myself running in circles sometimes as people chased me.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:16.000
And, like, staying the exact distance away from them where they can't hit me.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:19.380
And then every once in a while I would come up behind them and hit them once, keep running in circles.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:20.600
Like, that was, like, a strategy.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:25.740
Which games should never be, like, unbalanced enough that you have to, like, resort to things like that.

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:30.740
But I thought that the weapon thing was, like, the one thing that made me somewhat enjoy these levels.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.760
Where it was making me think, like, okay, I got a gun here.

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:34.700
Like, I can use it on this guy.

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:35.640
Okay, maybe I got to kill that guy.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:38.240
But I'll save it for this because I know this guy is coming up or whatever.

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:47.700
But, yeah. For the gun, I just, the way that I would describe the gun, I still think that the inclusion of it should be praised.

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:49.620
Because we're in a beat-em-up game.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:56.220
And, like, I haven't seen a gun in a beat-em-up that is supposed to be used, like, more situationally before.

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:59.740
I think it does add, like, alone.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:05.820
Like, the idea of this alone, like, sort of adds to the strategy of this game.

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:16.780
But the fact that the baseline combat is so, like, sluggish that it really undercuts that inclusion.

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:22.100
Like, the gun would be much cooler if this were a much more polished game.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.100
I don't think that it should be.

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:27.480
I do think that its inclusion should be praised in some way.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:32.580
And then also in these beat-em-up stages, there's the boss fights, too.

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:40.120
Where, you know, these guys have, I think they get a separate health bar on the opposite side of the screen of where your health bar is.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:44.300
And, you know, they're significantly bigger.

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:49.420
They have different ways of handling combat than, you know, your usual grunt.

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:57.620
Because I think, like, you know, the first boss you go up against in this beat-em-up stage actually, like, can pick you up and throw you and do a massive amount of damage.

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:00.640
And I feel like there's some real danger in that, too.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:08.200
Like, this feels like a little bit of a one-on-one style thing that we were talking about of, like, well, if you have a weapon here, that would be great.

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:12.240
Because then you can, you know, you can go through this fight a lot faster.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:17.640
But you also want to make sure you don't, like, just go, you know, like, just lose it or lose your opportunity.

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:20.200
Because, like, whenever you die in this game, you lose all your stuff, too.

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:31.500
So, you know, the actual boss fights here have a balance of, like, it's the same shitty combat that we've been talking about beforehand with all the different waves.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.100
But now it feels more personal.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:34.420
It feels like a one-on-one fight.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:43.440
I just wish that, like, I could be a little more creative than just trying to think of, like, ways to cheese it as I'm going up against these guys.

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:44.740
Because I'm being so cautious.

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:47.880
Because I'm realizing, like, you know, my health is a precious thing.

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:50.220
If I have a weapon, I really don't want to lose it.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:57.960
And so I'm just trying to really, like, circle around the area and then get, like, you know, I'll get, like, a punch or a kick in, maybe.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.300
And then I'll go back to, like, you know, running away.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:04.520
Maybe that's the way they, you know, they expected you to play out these boss fights.

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:08.100
Be more conservative and play out that way.

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:13.200
To me, it just felt like it doesn't match, like, how a beat-em-up usually goes down.

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:14.980
Right? Like, the pacing seemed off.

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:17.940
Yeah, it seems to be, like, less.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:21.440
I mean, it seems to be, like, similar amount of enemies on screen.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:29.540
But just because it takes so long to kill them, there ends up being fewer screens, kind of.

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:34.680
And you spend way more time, like, just staring at each individual section.

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:37.580
So, yeah, it definitely feels sluggish.

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:39.100
Joe, any thoughts on the boss fights?

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.360
Yeah, I mean, similarly, I mean, I just felt like it's...

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:46.660
I mean, I don't want to keep repeating myself.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:52.260
But, like, it takes several hits before you even see their health bar to start going down.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.260
Did you guys notice that? Yeah.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:56.660
Like, it's like they have an invisible health bar.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:00.280
Yeah, they have the colored bars they should have, you know, over each of the health bar.

00:25:00.280 --> 00:25:02.000
Yeah, it's like Sephiroth's invisible health bar.

00:25:02.200 --> 00:25:05.180
And it's just, like, things like that where it's like, well, why not at least give me the feedback?

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:09.480
I mean, I get that, like, there's a bulletproof vest in this game that kind of gives you that ability.

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:12.160
So, like, they must have that or something similar to that.

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:14.260
But it just, like, feels like, am I even doing anything?

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:18.060
I'm just sitting here, like, just hitting this boss over and over again while I'm getting destroyed.

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:19.820
And, like, I don't even have any feedback.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.360
So, like, it felt like a similar frustration there.

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:27.200
And I'm not sure what the answer is there, given the game that we currently have.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:31.060
Like, I'm not sure how you make the boss fights more engaging.

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:34.360
Because it is a scary threat to go up against.

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:39.920
And they're not, like, they're not impossible as, like, a one-on-one combat.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:46.600
I think that would suck if they were actually, like, also just, you know, really tough to beat.

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.840
And, you know, destroyed you no matter what you did.

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:52.120
Like, that would be my moment of giving up, too.

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:53.260
So, they are beatable.

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:55.180
And it's not terrible.

00:25:55.480 --> 00:26:00.620
But it's, like, it's not interesting enough for the types of bosses we're going up against.

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:07.280
I thought, like, you know, facing a gigantic dude who's clearly, like, can throw me over his head and suplex me.

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:08.940
Like, I thought that was a pretty cool idea.

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:10.620
It's just nothing really happens with it.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:12.400
It doesn't go anywhere.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:33.580
Right. So, the next one we'll talk about is the light gun shooting portion of the game.

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.760
Obviously, used within NES Zapper here.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:39.080
And just, you know, quick observation here.

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:41.440
I mean, last week we played Operation Wolf.

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:42.960
And that was made by Taito.

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.140
This game is made by Konami.

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:50.980
So, is it, like, did Nintendo play a role in, like, the release dates of these games?

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:53.980
And saying, like, oh, we should release Zapper games close to each other?

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:58.160
Or, like, I can't imagine Konami and Taito would want it this way.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:01.920
Where they'd be like, yeah, let's make two Zapper games and compete against each other.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.560
Like, it feels like Nintendo just kind of threw these in here.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:06.200
I don't know if you guys have any thoughts about that.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:12.580
Or if, like, this is, like, a weird point where, like, they're also both being used by a controller instead of the Zapper.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:17.240
If you so wish. Whereas, like, the Nintendo Zapper games are, like, you must use the Zapper.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:18.960
Any thoughts here on any of this stuff?

00:27:19.060 --> 00:27:20.660
I don't know if it was just a coincidence.

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:23.000
I think, like, the odds of it are coincidence.

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:35.220
But it is such an odd, like, because we, like, Zapper games have been so sporadically released recently that it's almost hard to believe that that's a coincidence.

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:43.540
Otherwise, I think it would definitely just be Nintendo of America saying, like, oh, we're going to do this.

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:46.900
Because they kind of, they fit that same mold.

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:56.420
Yeah, I wonder if, like, the Nintendo action set was being released, like, maybe, like, the Christmas before or something.

00:27:56.660 --> 00:27:58.980
Like, you know, that's the one that came with the Zapper in it.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:00.540
So maybe they're trying to, like, justify.

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.000
They're like, no, it wasn't a waste that you bought that.

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:05.660
Like, those games that you played, like, yeah, they're still good.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:07.320
But now you also have these new Zapper games.

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:09.740
Like, maybe they were trying to revitalize some Zapper interest.

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:12.860
But they really don't go anywhere else with it from here on out.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:15.960
So it just seems strange that we have back-to-back Zapper games.

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:17.920
But let's not talk about Operation Wolf.

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:20.440
Let's stay on topic here with Bayou Billy.

00:28:21.020 --> 00:28:25.700
Joe, you were a fan of these light gun portion of this game.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:28.220
And you said that maybe it could have even been a little longer.

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:29.840
What did you like so much about it?

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:36.960
Yeah, well, I mean, I think, like, first of all, I think it's clear that I am just a fan of Zapper levels and Zapper games.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:39.180
And I didn't play this with the Zapper.

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:43.840
So I think part of it was that I was very pleasantly surprised at how well it worked with the controller.

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:47.560
And I did listen to your episode last week that you guys were on where you both said the same thing.

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:50.080
So it was interesting to kind of experience that.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:52.940
But I don't know.

00:28:53.020 --> 00:29:01.220
I just thought it was, you know, it's not like it's different, like super different from, and I'm blanking on the name.

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:02.320
There was another game like this.

00:29:02.380 --> 00:29:04.500
The whole game was like this. We played, like, I think in 87.

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:09.420
I'm not going to remember. But it's not like it's super different than things that we've seen before.

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:14.260
But I just think it works really well, and it's nice for me to see, like.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:15.740
Were you thinking of Freedom Force?

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:17.320
Freedom Force. Yes, I was.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:21.980
Oh, yeah. Which I voted essential, if that's the one I'm thinking of.

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.000
You may have. Yeah.

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:25.540
Sounds like a Joe move.

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:35.040
And he's Appergame. But I just, you know, thought it was a nice way to, like, break up the adventure and make it feel like you're progressing.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.500
Make it feel like, you know, this is something a little different.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:46.140
And, admittedly, part of me was just having to imagine how cool it would be to just then bust out the Zapper in the middle of playing this other adventure and be like, all right, now I'm shooting people.

00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:49.540
But, yeah, I think it was very clean and it worked very well.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:30:01.200
And I love the fact that maybe this is something that we'll talk about later, but I love the fact that on the menu screen it allows you to go practice the Zapper.

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.220
You don't have to play through.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:05.200
You can, like, get better at that first before you play through the adventure.

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:08.300
So you can just play through the Zapper levels at any time you want.

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:15.940
And I thought that that was a really nice touch that kind of, like, makes it feel more like you're trying to get, you know, you're trying to progress.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.300
You know, you can be like, all right, I'm going to practice this before I start.

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:21.600
It's because I know that, you know, I'm not good at the Zapper game.

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:22.640
You can do this with other levels, too.

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:23.960
But, like, I'm not good at the Zapper levels.

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:24.900
I'm going to practice the Zapper levels.

00:30:25.040 --> 00:30:26.160
Okay, now I'm going to start my journey.

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:29.120
And you get to the Zapper level and now you feel like you've kind of earned it.

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:36.280
I'd say that these levels, like, yeah, it felt very much like the last game we played.

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:40.920
It was tight, like, with the controller controls because, again, I don't have a Zapper.

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:49.180
But I'd say that this had less going for it than remind me of the game we just played.

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:51.660
Operation Wolf? Operation Wolf.

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:59.020
Because Operation Wolf had, you know, the grenades, which you also had to keep track of.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:02.480
I don't think there was ammo in this one.

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:09.800
Was there ammo? There is ammo because if you run out of it, it's, again, it's another Operation Wolf parallel.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:14.240
They both have a thing where, like, if you lose, you can lose by running out of bullets.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:16.300
True. Okay. So not that.

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:21.500
But at least with Operation Wolf, it seemed like there was a bit more challenge.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:38.400
Because I think as challenging almost, yeah, very much to the point of frustration at the beat-em-up portions of this game where the first-person shooter, I guess, portions of this game were kind of easy for me.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:48.240
I don't know if it was because I was just doing it with a controller, but there were certain points where, like, the enemies are dropping in, like, two or three different areas.

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:52.340
And you could just kind of, like, oscillate between those two spawn spots.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:32:03.860
Sometimes there'll be enemies running by, but your cursor moves fast enough that you never really have too big of an issue catching up to them.

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:15.760
Um, so I thought that, like, this was, this is a bit more enjoyable, only so much that it felt more like a rest, um, as opposed to the beat-em-ups.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:18.600
But, yeah, I think that this was kind of half-baked, too.

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:24.520
I'd say the best part about these light gun levels are where the enemies can hide.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:31.800
Like, yes, for the most part, they're spawning, um, right out in the open, and it doesn't take much challenge to take those guys out.

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:35.640
But, you know, maybe my reaction times aren't what they used to be.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:39.580
But I liked, like, occasionally just being like, wait a minute, is that a guy in the bushes?

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:41.940
Like, you know, and, like, you barely see his head, you know?

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:46.200
Like, and then, like, you know, he probably took some damage on me or whatever.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:55.160
But, like, and then it's, like, satisfying to take those guys out because it's not, like, an entire, uh, you know, an entire human's body that you have access to.

00:32:55.260 --> 00:33:00.040
It's, like, you got to shoot the head and, like, they're, you know, they're in, like, the trees in the background.

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:06.420
I like how there was, like, a background, a bushes area, and then, like, the foreground, you know?

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:11.740
Like, I like that there was, like, multiple, even though it is all flat, obviously, and the zapper can't differentiate.

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:12.960
Like, it is kind of cool.

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:14.440
Like a classic shooting gallery.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:20.440
Right. You have to, like, pay attention to all areas of the, um, of it.

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:27.920
And I wasn't sure, uh, if they were just spawning randomly, like, you know, uh, each time or if they were being, like, properly placed.

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:31.620
Uh, Joe, I don't know if, you know, maybe the practice would have revealed that to you.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:33.380
Did, was it always the same placements?

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:35.940
I, don't quote me on this, but I thought that they were placed.

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:40.460
Like, I'm pretty sure I remember, like, redoing the level and starting to kind of, like, learn the patterns.

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:42.680
Um, but I'm not positive.

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:47.120
Yeah, because I was thinking about that even, like, when you get to the helicopter boss fight.

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:53.140
Those felt kind of random of when those soldiers, like, pop out of the helicopter or from the sides.

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:55.620
Um, so I wasn't sure.

00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:56.960
But, yeah, it would make sense.

00:33:57.040 --> 00:34:05.300
Placement would make a lot more sense for something like this because I imagine it would be hard to program, like, randomly showing up in the bushes or stuff like that.

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:10.920
Yeah, but, but I had, I had fun with the zapper portion of this game.

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:14.080
I did find it a little challenging, though.

00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:18.540
Not in the sense of, like, you know, game over screens all the time or anything like that.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:23.240
But, there's a lot of enemies on the screen, and I feel like, you know, if, let's say there's four, right?

00:34:23.780 --> 00:34:27.320
Even if you take out, like, three, you're probably going to get shot by one of them.

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:30.560
And that results in a loss of, you know, one health bar.

00:34:30.700 --> 00:34:37.540
So, I feel like that is, you know, along the course of a stage, that is kind of tough because they are hitting you as you keep going through.

00:34:37.580 --> 00:34:40.280
So, you do have to worry about it later on during the boss fights.

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:48.720
Like, I think it would be rare to go into the boss fights with a full health bar and, you know, not that you need it, but it would be helpful when dying counts as so much in this game.

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:57.260
Yeah, I think that, like, based on, based on previous experience, I think it might not necessarily be easier with the zapper, but I think it would be more doable with the zapper.

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:01.220
Like, because you don't, you're not limited to the speed that you move the cursor.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:03.220
It's just wherever you point, you can shoot.

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:07.760
So, if you're, if you're quick enough on your reflexes, I think with the zapper, that wouldn't be as much of a problem.

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:09.940
Yeah, just shoot point blank at the TV screen.

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:14.520
Yeah, yeah. I mean, if you don't cheat, then what I'm saying applies.

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:16.560
Hey, they're getting close to the TV screen.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:17.800
Why shouldn't you? That's true.

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:30.600
That's a very good point. But, yeah, I think overall, like, what you guys, well, especially what Sean's saying, too, kind of likens back to when I was saying that, like, at least the way I feel about it is it's a jack of all trades, master of none.

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.560
Like, I think that it does the light gun thing well.

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:39.660
But, yeah, it's not, it's not, like, as good as other light gun game things we've done before.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.160
But it's cool to see the variety.

00:35:42.040 --> 00:35:46.500
You know, it feels, this game feels a little bit like a little triathlon of games.

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:55.780
You know what I mean? And I feel like this, yes, it does give me a little bit of, like, I get to rest from that part that I'm, like, not crazy about.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:36:00.660
But also I'm definitely, like, on the edge of my seat, like, okay, I got to try and beat this stage this time.

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:13.820
You know, like, it feels like a fun challenge and not, like, the previous stage, the beat-em-up stage where it's, like, a little bit of, like, okay, I think I figured out a way to just cheese this because it's just way too, you know, way too difficult otherwise.

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:16.100
Does the game really need this, though?

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:19.440
Like, I think you know what I mean by saying that.

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:21.680
I'm not saying, like, it's good or bad or anything like that.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:24.960
Does it actually need this whole light gun shooting portion of it?

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:32.820
Like, is the game so long or such a slog that, like, oh, well, this will help, you know, keep people engaged and it's something different.

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:39.940
Like, I don't know, like, how much this feels like the rest of the game between the beat-em-up and the driving levels.

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:43.300
Like, how, I don't know, like, maybe it was the first-person view.

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:45.660
Maybe it was, like, the swamp setting.

00:36:46.580 --> 00:36:56.860
Maybe it was just, like, the idea that, like, a guy who was fighting with his fists a second ago and had nothing else available to him now has, like, a gun with, like, a plentiful amount of bullets.

00:36:57.440 --> 00:37:05.400
More so than you would expect from a guy who in the beat-em-up levels only gets, like, you know, I think it's, like, less than six shots with the gun.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:10.900
I think, I mean, yeah, I guess no game needs anything.

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:15.600
But I think this does add value to the experience.

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:20.680
Like, for somebody that has a light gun, the library for that's pretty small.

00:37:21.340 --> 00:37:26.240
You might want your light gun game to be more than just a light gun game.

00:37:26.900 --> 00:37:29.780
Um, I think it adds value.

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:31.740
I don't think... Not about adding value, though.

00:37:31.820 --> 00:37:34.620
I guess I just wonder, does it feel out of place in this game?

00:37:35.420 --> 00:37:38.200
Do you felt like it just was like, oh, yeah, now I'm going into the Zapper stage.

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:43.860
Like, of course. I guess, like, contextually, it maybe doesn't do, like, I don't know.

00:37:43.960 --> 00:37:47.840
I mean, even trying to think of, like, Golgo 13, like, how well that those were, like, split apart.

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:50.420
But, like, contextually, maybe it feels out of place.

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:53.300
But I think gameplay-wise, like, I don't know.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:54.880
I think the variety is the draw.

00:37:54.880 --> 00:38:08.920
Like, I think that without that variety of these, like, of these three totally different types of game, this would just completely melt into, like, the, like, super mediocre or even bad games that we've played.

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:11.440
Like, it's just another boring game.

00:38:11.540 --> 00:38:13.640
Where this made me interested in it, you know?

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:17.620
Not just the Zapper, but combined with the Zapper and the driving sections.

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:23.980
Right. At this point, they should have added, like, a high-stakes, you know, Texas Hold'em game into one of the levels, too, at the end of it.

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:25.660
Hey, I'm in for it. Like, now you're in my poker tournament.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:31.320
I'm in for it. You know, we've seen people try to do this before and, like, to varying degrees of success.

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:35.180
And I think that they did a decent job, minus the first level.

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:37.100
I just question the context is all.

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:40.240
I'm not saying, like, the Zapper levels are shit and they shouldn't be in the game.

00:38:40.240 --> 00:38:43.160
I just question why they're here.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:45.300
But a tremendous value to the customer.

00:38:45.580 --> 00:38:51.760
Yeah, I guess it's like having a, not like a Halo game and then there's a Kinect portion.

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:56.800
I guess, yeah, if you look at it that way, it's out of place.

00:38:58.020 --> 00:38:59.960
And I guess that means it's not needed.

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:12.460
But, yeah, I still think that in this sort of, like, not so mature video game space, it fits well enough.

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:16.200
But, Sean, you did make the best argument yet, which is the value thing.

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:22.180
Like, that is clearly something that, like, you know, is at the front of Konami's head, if not Nintendo's.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:25.440
It's like, you want to give a tremendous value for the game that you're buying.

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:30.720
You want people to think, like, I must buy this game because of everything it's bringing to the experience.

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:40.240
So, that is the most compelling reason of why to include a game that is a Zapper game, a normal, like, you know, beat-em-up style arcade game, and has, like, driving levels, too.

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:41.700
Like, yeah, that's definitely the sell.

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:44.240
When you read that in a magazine, you're like, I must buy this game.

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:45.360
It's three different games.

00:39:46.020 --> 00:39:54.340
Yeah. Yeah, and, you know, to address what you said a minute ago, Sean, about, like, the Kinect game being added to an Xbox game or something.

00:39:54.780 --> 00:40:01.380
The way that I sort of kind of – I guess it's just – it just depends on, like, your perspective on it or the way you like to look at it.

00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:08.860
But the way that I was sort of feeling about it was a little more like – like, we'll say a Ratchet & Clank game because those are just games that I know really well.

00:40:09.160 --> 00:40:12.160
Like, obviously, those games are all largely, like, one style of gameplay.

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:14.460
But, like, then you have the racing section.

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:18.640
And then you have the section where you get in a turret and you're suddenly, like, first person shooting down ships.

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:20.760
And then you have the section where you're on grind rail.

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:24.940
And, like, they're all different little, like, mini games within the game that help get you there.

00:40:24.940 --> 00:40:27.000
I think the difference there, though, is that you're using the same input method.

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:28.520
Like, it's always a controller.

00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:34.900
So everything feels a bit more fluid because you don't have to run and – not run and grab.

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:37.760
But, like, now you don't have to put your controller down and grab the gun.

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:39.120
And now you're shooting with the gun.

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:42.020
It feels a little bit more disjointed.

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:46.780
And I guess as I keep talking, I'm siding more with Mike on this.

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:54.560
But the mixed input method, I think, is where that feeling of disjointedness kind of comes from.

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:57.340
Not so much different modes of play.

00:40:57.940 --> 00:40:59.500
Yeah. Okay. I mean, I see what you're saying.

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:02.660
I'm, like, yeah. I'm, like, actually, like, physically, like, having different controllers.

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:08.600
But then personally, I feel like that that is a minimal – like, that's still – I'm not saying that they did it perfectly.

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:14.680
But now just the idea, the concept of, like, switching input methods doesn't bother me if it's done right.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:15.880
Which I think this was done okay.

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:22.420
Yeah. Could have been done better. But, like, if I see a game done really well where you have to put down your controller and pick up, like, a gun, I think that could be a really cool thing.

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:24.200
You know, I don't think that that's necessarily a negative.

00:41:31.180 --> 00:41:36.600
And you'll have to change inputs again, Joe, because we are now talking about the driving levels of the game.

00:41:37.520 --> 00:41:40.700
You know, I think driving levels, you know, we were talking about this.

00:41:40.840 --> 00:41:42.380
They're definitely not racing levels in my book.

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:43.620
You're not racing anybody.

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:47.840
Could be a race against your gas, though, as Sean mentioned earlier on in the episode.

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:50.360
It's just – it's like mock rider stages, you know?

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:57.220
They have this fake 3D look to them and the roads bend at a moment's notice.

00:41:57.220 --> 00:41:58.340
But I think you can tell.

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:07.580
Actually, I would argue that you can tell here more so than in any other one of these games that we've played that, you know, that the road is about to bend.

00:42:07.660 --> 00:42:20.120
And I actually found myself enjoying the driving portions of this game specifically because there's, like, enough control over your car.

00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:30.400
There's enough, like – there's not too many other vehicles littered, so you have time to shoot them because you have – they're saying it's grenades, but I thought it was just bullets from the car.

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:32.460
Do we have a verdict on whether it's grenades or bullets?

00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:34.320
I think it's grenades.

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:46.720
That's interesting. You're just – yeah, you have a car equipped with shooting grenades in New Orleans, and I just liked how much control you had over your environment and over the other vehicles.

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:59.760
And then it all comes crashing down when you realize that, like, oh, I made it through, like, 25% of the stage, and even though I unfortunately did crash into a pole that I didn't see, you know, I get to start right over again.

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:01.640
So let me just keep going along, you know?

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:03.040
Like, I get to start right where I am.

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:05.160
So now I go a little bit further.

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:08.920
I maybe get to, like, the 40% mark, and that's being generous.

00:43:09.100 --> 00:43:11.860
And then I hit another pole, and it's like, all right, simple mistake.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:13.140
Like, I see what I did there.

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:14.240
I'm going to fix this.

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:18.040
Just respawn me back in, and it does that, and that's really nice.

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:24.120
I'm like, wow, I can't believe this game is just going to, like, allow me to just pick up right where I left off because I have the lives for it.

00:43:24.520 --> 00:43:26.560
And then all of a sudden you don't have the lives for it anymore.

00:43:26.680 --> 00:43:30.720
You realize you made it maybe 50% of the way through or 60% or whatever.

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:33.300
And you're like, all right, no big deal.

00:43:33.460 --> 00:43:34.300
I'll just continue.

00:43:34.540 --> 00:43:37.260
Hopefully the game doesn't start you all over again.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:39.700
And they don't. And you're like, oh, that's a relief.

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:41.460
But they start you back at the beginning of the level.

00:43:42.440 --> 00:43:44.600
And it's like, well, that was a really long stage.

00:43:44.880 --> 00:43:50.380
Am I going to really remember all of the twists and turns and all of the pulls I hit from earlier on?

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:51.840
Am I going to be able to be perfect? You have to remember that?

00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:54.980
Am I going to be able to be perfect for this long?

00:43:55.260 --> 00:44:00.400
I don't know. It felt like the length of this stage was what hurt it from being fun.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:02.720
I mean, yeah.

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:04.860
I guess they are a little long.

00:44:05.460 --> 00:44:13.860
I felt like a lot of the obstacles were easy enough to avoid.

00:44:13.860 --> 00:44:31.520
If you're prioritizing avoiding things that are going to make you explode and maybe allowing yourself to take a hit from, I don't know, a puddle or oil slick or whatever the hell those things are that just slows you down a bit.

00:44:31.520 --> 00:44:34.420
I thought that it was manageable enough.

00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:43.140
You could sort of juke the helicopters to drop stuff on the sides of the road.

00:44:43.540 --> 00:44:44.620
What are they dropping?

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:46.240
I don't even know.

00:44:46.540 --> 00:44:47.960
They're like torpedoes or something.

00:44:49.480 --> 00:44:50.800
But I don't know.

00:44:50.840 --> 00:44:52.860
I didn't find these too challenging.

00:44:53.140 --> 00:44:57.900
There are some frustrating moments just with fuel or time, whatever you want to call it.

00:44:57.900 --> 00:45:04.380
But I also didn't see anything great about them either.

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:06.720
I mean, I guess it's got a bit of a sense of speed.

00:45:06.740 --> 00:45:10.020
I don't know. Yeah.

00:45:10.100 --> 00:45:13.540
I mean, once again, I really didn't find this too long.

00:45:14.720 --> 00:45:15.700
It was long.

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:19.640
And I see what you're saying about having to start the levels over after you lose all your lives.

00:45:19.940 --> 00:45:22.260
But I thought that that was a good balance.

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:29.240
I mean, it didn't just put you back right where you died, but didn't send you back to the beginning of the game, which so many games have done so far.

00:45:29.380 --> 00:45:37.200
And it is, yeah, it is like you do have to sort of practice and get better at this, which, again, they include in the menu.

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:39.540
But I thought that this was a fun addition.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:50.800
And I agree that it wasn't like there wasn't anything like absolutely amazing about the driving compared to like a game that would maybe focus solely on this kind of driving.

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:55.840
But it was in a game that has a lot of other things in it.

00:45:55.920 --> 00:45:58.640
Like I thought it was another fun switch up.

00:45:58.880 --> 00:46:00.440
And it didn't overstay its welcome for me.

00:46:00.520 --> 00:46:06.700
Like I'm sure I would have gotten bored because they would have needed to add a little more to it if they wanted to make a full game out of it.

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:12.960
But for being like two levels in this game, I enjoyed these parts.

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:14.980
No, I love the stages.

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:17.540
Don't get me wrong. Like I love the driving levels.

00:46:17.700 --> 00:46:19.340
And I think they are a good challenge.

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:27.220
I question, you know, my gameplay abilities, I suppose, because I did find these challenging.

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:33.380
I found the driving levels to be, you know, not unforgiving all the time.

00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:39.680
You know, it's not one of those things where I'm constantly dying or an enemy spawns out of nowhere and I crash into him.

00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:48.740
Or I don't even think there's like, you know, there's not like the Mock Rider style obstacles of like puddles and other things that make you like slip and slide around.

00:46:48.900 --> 00:47:02.620
So like it's generally a relaxing experience compared to, you know, the reflex moves of the light gun shooting part or just the slow draw of the beat-em-up stages.

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:03.880
So I really like these.

00:47:04.060 --> 00:47:11.420
I guess I just question my own gameplay ability because I found myself to be struggling with these driving levels over the course of the entire level.

00:47:11.620 --> 00:47:15.640
You know, enough that like – You're talking to somebody that had to cheat for the beat-em-up portion.

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:16.520
So I think you're fine.

00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:19.420
Yeah. And also, I mean, I definitely struggled too.

00:47:19.480 --> 00:47:21.240
And then I started practicing over and over.

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:29.380
You know, like that is another thing about the practice where it makes you – I feel like it makes you feel like this is like – it makes your goals more like tangible.

00:47:29.480 --> 00:47:36.560
It makes you feel like, okay, I'm going to try and beat this game but not like other games where it's like I just have to keep trying over and over again and just get better like that tedious way.

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:38.720
But it's like, okay, I know I'm bad at driving parts.

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:43.480
I can just go play the driving parts and get better at those before I play the game.

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:45.560
And I don't know. That just gave it a little more like – I don't know.

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:48.260
Like a cooler feeling of progress to me.

00:47:48.520 --> 00:47:50.860
I really should have done the practice mode stuff.

00:47:50.860 --> 00:47:53.960
You keep bringing it up, Joe, but I think we should just mention what it is real fast.

00:47:54.080 --> 00:48:04.680
It's another game mode that you can select from the main menu that basically is like a shorter version of select stages that lets you like figure out how to play.

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:08.860
Like, oh, I'm not good with the zapper so I want to get better or I want to get better at driving.

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:16.780
So there's shorter stages but then when you complete these practice stages, you are awarded a power-up that can be used in the actual game.

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:23.100
And that's a pretty cool bonus for like – it's almost like a hidden feature that shouldn't be so hidden.

00:48:23.140 --> 00:48:24.880
I'm sure it's mentioned in the manual or whatever.

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:31.160
But like that seems very helpful to any player who's going through this game because you're probably going to need those power-ups.

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:39.200
Yeah, no, I thought that that was a cool – because usually these like – we've played games with practice modes.

00:48:40.180 --> 00:48:42.700
But they're usually just like an afterthought slapped on.

00:48:43.700 --> 00:48:59.100
And it's a very simple thing that adds to their inclusion of, yeah, just adding that stat boost or that extra life or whatever the rewards are to make it feel more a part of the whole package.

00:48:59.540 --> 00:49:00.680
I really like that.

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:04.120
Now, Joe, you're the one who played practice mode so I want to hear your thoughts.

00:49:04.260 --> 00:49:06.140
Oh, I did too. Oh, okay.

00:49:06.260 --> 00:49:07.840
Sorry. Joe just keeps bringing it up.

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:12.480
You can't shut up. Yeah, I literally have said it on every section of the podcast so far.

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:15.660
No, yeah, like I said, I think I've said my piece about it.

00:49:15.800 --> 00:49:24.260
I think that it adds a lot to the game and it gives you also just the option to just play your favorite type of level because the levels are so different.

00:49:24.420 --> 00:49:31.280
I mean that might not necessarily be like – depending on how you want to look at that, that can be a plus, like a positive or a negative.

00:49:31.400 --> 00:49:36.280
I mean if it's like you just hate the rest of the game and you only want to play that part, that doesn't speak well to the game.

00:49:36.280 --> 00:49:45.000
But if it's like, oh, my favorite part is the first-person shooter parts or like I want to have a friend over to play a Zapper game, you can just jump into the Zapper game.

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:45.920
I think that's really neat.

00:49:46.080 --> 00:49:57.520
I definitely like the reward thing though and I almost wonder if like should that have been a – should that have been something in this game?

00:49:57.520 --> 00:50:11.100
In the main modes too though of like the way that you get the power-ups in this game mostly are in the beat-em-up stages just by like you punch an enemy and then maybe a turkey falls out of him.

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:14.100
It sounds ridiculous but that's what happens.

00:50:14.800 --> 00:50:17.840
That's how you find most of the power-ups in this game.

00:50:18.100 --> 00:50:21.180
Other times it's like you shoot in the light gun stages.

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:24.580
You shoot the guy and then it's like, oh, there's a bulletproof vest so you collect that.

00:50:24.740 --> 00:50:29.980
You know, like cool. All those things check out and they're typical for video games.

00:50:30.080 --> 00:50:44.600
But I wonder if there was like another incentive of like a certain random thing or not random or based on performance that you could get to help you with the next stage similar to the practice mode.

00:50:44.600 --> 00:50:53.020
It seems like it's a feature already built in like that they can make that work so it would have probably enhanced playthroughs of like, you know, how well did you do during the beat-em-up stage?

00:50:53.160 --> 00:50:55.140
Well, now you have this in the light gun round.

00:50:55.720 --> 00:50:58.140
Yeah. I think – yeah.

00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:08.520
I mean I think that first of all, it's cool that they include power-ups there but it would be – yeah, it would be really cool if they made it like almost more integral to the overall.

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:12.920
Yeah, because the power-up thing, like the overall situation of the power-ups is just strange in general.

00:51:13.120 --> 00:51:14.600
It's – you know, we're even calling them power-ups.

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:15.780
We probably should just call them items.

00:51:15.980 --> 00:51:24.560
Yeah. But like, you know, dying at any point in the game results in the loss of whatever weapon you had and the bulletproof vest.

00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:27.420
Now, if you have both, you'll lose both.

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.340
If you have either, you'll lose either one you have.

00:51:29.380 --> 00:51:32.360
It's very simple. No matter what, you're losing these things anytime you die.

00:51:32.500 --> 00:51:36.040
But this also counts in levels where they have no effect.

00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:47.120
So if you had a weapon that you were carrying over from like a light gun stage to like a later beat-em-up stage, well, it doesn't matter if you die during the light gun stage because now that weapon is gone too.

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:55.560
I guess like logically that checks out but continuity in general would end for that person if they died.

00:51:55.720 --> 00:51:56.960
It's true. Like what they did.

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:58.680
Your episodic memory stops.

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:01.620
Yeah, like they blacked out and they were like, oh, cool.

00:52:01.720 --> 00:52:04.200
Let's take his knife and then they were like, all right, but let him get back up.

00:52:05.420 --> 00:52:14.640
No, no, no. I think dying in a video game is like, you know, you're playing a different version of this person in the multiverse.

00:52:14.980 --> 00:52:16.160
So –

00:52:16.160 --> 00:52:20.480
Who also did everything exactly the same before that though minus having the knife?

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:22.200
Yes. Oh, okay.

00:52:22.200 --> 00:52:24.360
Yes, okay. Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:27.300
That checks out for my explanation.

00:52:27.800 --> 00:52:29.940
Yeah, see, I actually didn't realize that that's how it worked though.

00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:40.660
That like in the shooting level if I died that I would lose – I thought it was actually just every time you went to a new level you lost your older power-ups because I always died at the next level.

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:44.780
So I never felt the other – I never experienced the other way around where I got –

00:52:44.780 --> 00:52:45.760
The carry over, right?

00:52:45.760 --> 00:52:52.160
Yeah, I never got through like the shoot-em-up and then the driving level without dying and then got back to a beat-em-up and was like, hey, I still got my weapons.

00:52:54.720 --> 00:53:00.080
You know, it's just a neat game idea for the people who make games, who have made games for us.

00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:01.520
I mean we had a game jam earlier this year.

00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:03.040
Check out that episode and play those games.

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:14.740
If anybody wants to do this, make a platforming game where like anytime you fall into a hole or die by an enemy, you like immediately respawn to where you were like five seconds before that.

00:53:14.740 --> 00:53:24.760
And then once you beat the game, during the credits, it shows you your playthrough where you were the one who was perfect, like who never made any of the mistakes and it just shows you your entire playthrough.

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:28.000
I think that would be amazing. I feel like there is a game like that.

00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:31.460
It kind of sounds like Super Meat Boy but that shows all of your deaths too.

00:53:32.640 --> 00:53:33.760
But no, do it.

00:53:33.880 --> 00:53:35.720
Like even if it's not been done before, do it.

00:53:36.280 --> 00:53:37.680
Do it. There you go.

00:53:40.900 --> 00:53:42.920
Godfather Gordon or whatever his name was.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:45.180
His laugh in the cut scenes.

00:53:45.520 --> 00:53:50.620
Go ahead. You know, like the synthetic laugh that they created.

00:53:50.620 --> 00:53:53.380
And also for the game itself, for the game title.

00:53:54.760 --> 00:53:58.120
All really cool stuff that whenever there's voice synthetic stuff, I like it.

00:53:58.260 --> 00:54:01.980
I just thought the laugh here was just something worth calling attention to.

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:03.600
It was very strange.

00:54:04.960 --> 00:54:08.900
It wasn't like a, you know, it wasn't like a human laugh.

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:15.000
I can't imagine it right now in my head, but I did notice that there was some cool voice stuff in the game.

00:54:15.020 --> 00:54:16.340
I'll play it back for people here.

00:54:17.860 --> 00:54:18.960
But that sounds great.

00:54:19.260 --> 00:54:24.440
What you just played, like for the audience, that was very disturbing.

00:54:24.780 --> 00:54:25.800
So I agree.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:27.600
Joe says it sounds great.

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:31.860
No, not that. I mean the Bayou Billy, like that's what I said at the top, right?

00:54:32.180 --> 00:54:39.240
Yeah. Yeah, yeah. That one sounded like we've come a long way, I think, from Bubble Bibble back when we played that game.

00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:41.560
Yeah, but you know what?

00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:44.360
The Intellivoice, which was an add-on for the Intellivision.

00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:47.560
Now that was a really good voice synthesis system.

00:54:47.740 --> 00:54:48.800
And that had some really cool stuff.

00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:53.240
But it was actually like you had to plug the Intellivoice into the cartridge system of the Intellivision.

00:54:53.360 --> 00:54:56.220
And then plug the Intellivision cartridge into your Intellivoice.

00:54:56.220 --> 00:55:03.100
And then all of a sudden your games would now have, your older games, keep in mind, would now have voice functionality added to them.

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:08.400
That's crazy. You mean like you could tell it, you could just say like left and you'd go left or something?

00:55:09.220 --> 00:55:11.800
Like it would have voices now in it.

00:55:11.960 --> 00:55:15.560
But where would the voices, like who developed those voices for the games?

00:55:15.560 --> 00:55:16.980
Other games. Yeah, yeah.

00:55:17.060 --> 00:55:18.280
Where are the other ghosts in the machine?

00:55:18.460 --> 00:55:23.720
Yeah, you're right. Where's the IntelliSmell that makes BurgerTime?

00:55:23.860 --> 00:55:25.420
This is where you can smell the burgers you're making.

00:55:26.980 --> 00:55:28.680
BurgerTime was a fever dream by the chef.

00:55:28.820 --> 00:55:29.960
Don't forget Sean said it first.

00:55:31.180 --> 00:55:33.860
All right. Also, a quick observation here.

00:55:34.680 --> 00:55:41.560
Avoiding damage during one of the light gun boss fights, the one before you get to Godfather Gordon,

00:55:42.700 --> 00:55:46.760
it's impossible to avoid damage during that particular boss fight.

00:55:46.980 --> 00:55:53.520
If you reach the machine gunman and his knife-throwing partner without enough health, you will die.

00:55:53.820 --> 00:55:54.740
Like you're dead.

00:55:55.040 --> 00:55:56.120
Like it's predetermined.

00:55:56.620 --> 00:55:57.600
It is written.

00:55:58.020 --> 00:56:01.120
It is written because you have to take a certain amount of hits.

00:56:01.360 --> 00:56:02.460
You're forced to.

00:56:02.680 --> 00:56:07.640
Like there's no way to – even like I guess with the best equipment, you know, hackers have,

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:13.820
there's no way to dodge taking equipment, taking damage because, you know,

00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:17.700
like the light gun does the whole like register of the flash and hitting you and everything like that.

00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:20.880
And these ones are like forced into the gameplay.

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:22.640
So you must take that damage.

00:56:22.800 --> 00:56:25.120
So if you don't come in with enough health, you're dead.

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:27.300
Huh. It's not your fault.

00:56:27.620 --> 00:56:28.940
Still like those light gun levels, Joe?

00:56:29.080 --> 00:56:30.600
I didn't think so. Yeah.

00:56:31.080 --> 00:56:32.080
I didn't know about this.

00:56:32.080 --> 00:56:36.560
So maybe that's – maybe that's – I was better off ignorant.

00:56:36.820 --> 00:56:37.720
You know, ignorance is bliss.

00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:40.260
What are your other thoughts on this game, Joe?

00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:41.680
Any stray thoughts?

00:56:42.280 --> 00:56:45.920
Well, I have a stray thought if we want to kind of move away from the game itself.

00:56:46.480 --> 00:56:50.420
Yeah, go for it. And that is that there is – and I'm trying to remember what it's called on the menu screen.

00:56:50.420 --> 00:56:54.980
There is a mode called music mode or sound mode.

00:56:55.080 --> 00:56:56.180
Like a sound mode or something like that.

00:56:56.240 --> 00:56:57.040
Sound mode.

00:56:57.040 --> 00:57:04.700
And that mode is just something I would have loved as a kid, maybe not particularly for this game, but for like all of my games,

00:57:04.720 --> 00:57:10.240
where it literally just lets you go in there and select any music track in the game and just listen to it.

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:12.600
Not only that, like sound effects too.

00:57:12.820 --> 00:57:19.840
Yeah, sound effects and like the little voice files and the laugh and like literally every sound that's in this game, which that's cool.

00:57:19.980 --> 00:57:21.980
I mean that's neat if you're interested in it when you're a kid.

00:57:21.980 --> 00:57:23.220
But also the music –

00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:27.900
I think it was just designed for kids to make like prank calls and use that as like a sound board.

00:57:28.180 --> 00:57:29.080
Yeah, perfect.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:30.860
That adds more value to this game.

00:57:32.820 --> 00:57:41.620
But I mean the amount of times when I was a kid that I would like turn on – I would go into the ice path in Pokemon Gold and just listen to the music.

00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:50.980
Or like I would play like some Game Boy game or a PlayStation game and just turn on – turn sound effects off and just listen to that music.

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:53.020
I mean nowadays it doesn't really matter.

00:57:53.100 --> 00:57:56.580
You can look anything up. You can find everything on YouTube or you can find it on Spotify or whatever.

00:57:56.980 --> 00:57:59.320
But like back then it was – that would have been a really cool feature.

00:57:59.500 --> 00:58:04.160
I would just argue that, yeah, I love sound effect modes and they should be all over the place.

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:09.860
In any game you should be able to select any of the music you want to hear and the sound effects and stuff like that.

00:58:09.860 --> 00:58:14.200
But a lot of times it's hidden in games and maybe like the devs hit it somewhere.

00:58:14.360 --> 00:58:18.040
But like you have to like put in a certain input on the main menu screen to find it.

00:58:18.120 --> 00:58:18.960
It's like just screw that.

00:58:19.120 --> 00:58:20.340
Just put it out there, man.

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:23.200
Like just put it on the freaking menu and let me select it.

00:58:23.540 --> 00:58:24.340
Yeah, do it.

00:58:24.620 --> 00:58:26.040
Do it. Sean, what about you?

00:58:26.180 --> 00:58:27.060
Any straight thoughts?

00:58:29.620 --> 00:58:30.740
I don't know.

00:58:30.800 --> 00:58:32.560
I like Bourbon Street.

00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:37.580
It kind of reminds me of – that's just what Bourbon Street is like is people picking fights.

00:58:37.580 --> 00:58:38.820
Well, you know what?

00:58:38.820 --> 00:58:42.660
You bring up a good point though, Sean, about one particular thing, okay?

00:58:43.680 --> 00:58:50.040
Does Godfather Gordon run New Orleans so much that like no civilians should be about?

00:58:50.500 --> 00:58:55.580
Like even in the light gun levels, there's no like gotcha people that you're not supposed to shoot.

00:58:55.740 --> 00:58:58.480
Like you're supposed to just kill everything you run into in this game.

00:58:58.480 --> 00:59:05.860
It's probably like – it's obviously nighttime when the game is happening and we all know that Bourbon Street clears out at like sundown.

00:59:06.780 --> 00:59:08.420
So it's very realistic.

00:59:09.360 --> 00:59:13.940
Yep, yep. I think it just makes Godfather Gordon really an intimidating villain.

00:59:14.120 --> 00:59:15.920
You're like, oh my – like the world building there.

00:59:16.120 --> 00:59:18.000
Yeah. How much everyone's afraid of him.

00:59:19.640 --> 00:59:21.760
Godfather Gordon should really have his own game too.

00:59:21.880 --> 00:59:23.020
I'd like to hear more about him.

00:59:23.840 --> 00:59:25.400
Yeah. Or a movie.

00:59:29.300 --> 00:59:30.580
Sequels and spinoffs.

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:32.780
Did you think that there was going to be a section for this?

00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:34.300
Is there a Godfather Gordon game?

00:59:34.400 --> 00:59:35.820
Please say there are none.

00:59:37.060 --> 00:59:44.960
There are games or rather media in the Adventures of Bayou Billy cinematic universe.

00:59:45.500 --> 00:59:51.440
Okay. So the first thing we need to talk about though is that there is a Famicom game called Mad City.

00:59:52.020 --> 00:59:53.080
Yes. Okay. Yeah.

00:59:53.180 --> 00:59:54.560
I'm surprised we haven't talked about this yet.

00:59:54.560 --> 01:00:07.380
Yeah. Mad City is this game but the Japanese version of this game and the NES version is just a harder version in every which way you can imagine of this game called Mad City.

01:00:07.900 --> 01:00:12.440
So enemies in the beat-em-up stages have – they have less health.

01:00:12.700 --> 01:00:14.040
They don't attack as frequently.

01:00:15.280 --> 01:00:17.160
It's not Bayou-centric.

01:00:18.020 --> 01:00:19.480
Yeah. It's not Bayou-centric.

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:21.600
We don't have – but even like even just gameplay-wise.

01:00:21.700 --> 01:00:22.640
I'm just going over the gameplay here.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:27.100
Like you have more ammunition in the light gun stages in Mad City.

01:00:29.360 --> 01:00:31.000
The driving stages.

01:00:31.220 --> 01:00:33.100
Like the road is actually wider.

01:00:33.620 --> 01:00:35.780
Like there's more room to be spread out.

01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:50.080
It's like whoever – like whenever they brought this over, they were either like angry at Americans or they thought like Americans are really good at video games because they just made every possible decision along the way to make this a harder game gameplay-wise.

01:00:50.080 --> 01:00:52.420
That seems to be like a common thread though.

01:00:52.600 --> 01:00:58.700
Like usually when games – I mean not always but it's never like it gets easier crossing the ocean.

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:04.520
It's always like the American version – the American port is always harder for some reason.

01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:13.100
I don't know. Is it – I would have thought – the only example I can think of is Mario 2, the original Japanese Mario 2.

01:01:13.540 --> 01:01:18.040
Wasn't that like not even brought here because they thought like Americans aren't good enough at video games to play this game?

01:01:18.040 --> 01:01:20.320
Yeah, but Nintendo America, we're the only people smart enough.

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:21.500
I think Sean is right here.

01:01:21.600 --> 01:01:28.480
I think most of the time when we get these ports, we find out that the Japanese one actually had like two modes, easy and hard or something.

01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:29.840
You know like – Oh, interesting.

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:32.460
We usually just get the hard mode for some reason.

01:01:32.660 --> 01:01:38.800
I also make gross generalities – gross generalizations that end up wrong pretty often on this podcast.

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:40.540
So it could be either way.

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:45.240
Now, Mad City does lose all the New Orleans stuff and everything like that.

01:01:45.260 --> 01:01:47.720
But the general idea is still the same.

01:01:48.040 --> 01:01:58.660
But the Famicom version has four possible endings and the NES one only has the one normal ending where you save Annabelle and get the kiss and all that.

01:01:58.820 --> 01:02:02.320
So blah, blah, blah. That's cute and everything and that's the normal ending that one should expect.

01:02:02.460 --> 01:02:05.520
But there's three other endings that you could get here.

01:02:06.340 --> 01:02:10.380
After beating the last boss, Annabelle comes out of a door.

01:02:10.560 --> 01:02:11.720
She walks over to you.

01:02:11.720 --> 01:02:16.120
And in Mad City, you can move around during this scene.

01:02:16.460 --> 01:02:17.320
Like run away from her?

01:02:17.820 --> 01:02:27.420
Yeah. If you run away from her and stay away from her long enough, you'll get an alternate ending where Annabelle decides that Billy no longer wants her and she goes her separate way.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:28.580
That's crazy.

01:02:28.960 --> 01:02:32.920
Why? Not something you're allowed to do in the NES version.

01:02:33.980 --> 01:02:35.180
Another one, okay?

01:02:35.960 --> 01:02:37.480
This one cracks me up.

01:02:38.300 --> 01:02:50.640
The ending that – this ending gives you that after you have beaten the game, it informs you that you have cheated because you used the power-ups that you gained from the practice mode.

01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:55.380
Oh, come on. And therefore refuses to show you any ending at all.

01:02:55.380 --> 01:02:57.620
It just says the end in white text.

01:02:58.040 --> 01:03:00.000
That's so – that's evil.

01:03:00.320 --> 01:03:04.820
Yeah, that's bullshit because that's there like to entice you to do that stuff.

01:03:04.880 --> 01:03:05.900
They gave you those. Yeah.

01:03:05.980 --> 01:03:07.460
But now they're saying, no, you cheated.

01:03:07.460 --> 01:03:08.780
You had these power-ups.

01:03:08.860 --> 01:03:09.900
You just get the end.

01:03:09.900 --> 01:03:11.380
You took this government handout.

01:03:12.560 --> 01:03:18.240
Yeah, yeah. And then the last one, which again, just amazing that there's even this many endings.

01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:31.700
If you hold the up button on the D-pad and the select button during the ending, during mid-scene, Annabelle and Billy will change the dialect of Japanese that they are speaking.

01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:44.080
And Billy will ask Annabelle for a kiss to which she turns to the player and implies that she knows that they are watching and she cannot kiss Billy.

01:03:44.240 --> 01:03:45.440
I actually just got chills.

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:50.700
She knows that other people are watching us.

01:03:50.880 --> 01:03:51.920
Wait, that we're watching?

01:03:52.080 --> 01:03:53.660
Oh, that's creepy. That we're watching.

01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:55.100
That's some Kojima shit right there.

01:03:55.780 --> 01:04:03.980
And it's like, yeah, but why is it if she can see us, why isn't she afraid that like we've been possessing her boyfriend this entire game to begin with?

01:04:04.120 --> 01:04:08.600
Well, because now you're like she's in physical contact with them and she can feel it.

01:04:09.400 --> 01:04:10.760
I think that she's telling him.

01:04:10.860 --> 01:04:12.060
I think that she's always known.

01:04:12.160 --> 01:04:13.300
Like maybe they all always know.

01:04:13.360 --> 01:04:14.760
And then he's like, shut up.

01:04:14.840 --> 01:04:16.480
Like, don't let them know.

01:04:16.660 --> 01:04:26.020
We know they're here. You know, it's like very like like they have to smile and like grin, bear their teeth through it and like knowing that like every moment is this horror for them.

01:04:26.760 --> 01:04:28.440
They're like, no, we're video game characters.

01:04:28.600 --> 01:04:29.740
We have to stick to the script.

01:04:30.080 --> 01:04:31.140
And they just keep going.

01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:32.820
Or he'll turn the game off.

01:04:33.140 --> 01:04:41.000
Yeah. There's also a comic book series called The Adventures of Bayou Billy, believe it or not.

01:04:41.000 --> 01:04:43.000
And it was released by Archie Comics.

01:04:43.300 --> 01:04:51.160
And I think it's notable for being the first video game like actual like, you know, like video game comic book series.

01:04:51.520 --> 01:04:53.600
Did it have a long run? What's that?

01:04:53.660 --> 01:04:54.600
Did it have a long run?

01:04:54.860 --> 01:04:55.860
No, it did not.

01:04:56.080 --> 01:04:58.280
I think like eight issues, maybe six.

01:04:58.980 --> 01:05:02.580
But it came before Sonic the Hedgehog got his Archie Comics.

01:05:03.100 --> 01:05:10.860
So that's just kind of crazy that like when, you know, when they're looking for video games to adapt and turn into comics, they went to Bayou Billy first.

01:05:10.860 --> 01:05:18.140
Wow. And then also Bayou Billy appears in an episode of Captain N, the Game Master.

01:05:18.140 --> 01:05:19.880
We haven't talked about Captain N in a while.

01:05:20.140 --> 01:05:22.540
The episode was called How's Bayou.

01:05:22.540 --> 01:05:24.220
I don't know the pun there.

01:05:25.760 --> 01:05:26.920
How is you?

01:05:27.320 --> 01:05:28.260
How is, yeah.

01:05:28.340 --> 01:05:30.680
How's Bayou. How's about you?

01:05:31.680 --> 01:05:32.500
Oh, my God.

01:05:33.200 --> 01:05:35.620
This is like a live, like figuring it out.

01:05:35.620 --> 01:05:36.780
It's like another game.

01:05:38.660 --> 01:05:46.860
Playing on the difficulty of its real world counterpart, Bayou Billy was said to be the one game that even Captain N was unable to conquer.

01:05:50.180 --> 01:05:51.520
That's kind of funny, right?

01:05:51.700 --> 01:05:53.360
Captain N, he's the video game master.

01:05:53.500 --> 01:05:55.820
He's like, yeah, I can't beat the Adventures of Bayou Billy.

01:05:56.040 --> 01:06:01.700
Yeah, whatever, man. And then a sentence nobody should really ever have to say, but I'm going to say it.

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:02.700
It's the plot of the episode.

01:06:03.400 --> 01:06:04.940
Mother Brain had Dr.

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:10.900
Wily build a robot cat to have Duke lead Captain N to Bayou Billy's world.

01:06:11.460 --> 01:06:15.540
Captain N ended up meeting Bayou Billy where he learned some tricks from him.

01:06:16.660 --> 01:06:20.820
I feel like we didn't learn a damn thing and that's way too many characters in that first sentence.

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:22.640
That was like word salad to me.

01:06:22.720 --> 01:06:24.580
I don't even know what you meant by that.

01:06:24.580 --> 01:06:31.060
Yeah, it was like you plugged in a bunch of video game stuff into like an AI that's supposed to deliver a description.

01:06:31.060 --> 01:06:32.820
Did you write Ready Player One?

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:36.660
Too good.

01:06:38.840 --> 01:06:40.080
Yeah, I didn't.

01:06:40.300 --> 01:06:41.860
But it seems like I could have.

01:06:41.980 --> 01:06:43.620
You could have. Based on that sentence.

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:52.220
You know, Joe, you mentioned AI algorithms and one thing that AI algorithms cannot figure out is where or whether.

01:06:52.480 --> 01:06:53.780
What? No, I'm sorry.

01:06:54.020 --> 01:06:55.240
Go ahead. What did you say to me?

01:06:55.380 --> 01:06:56.440
Did he say that?

01:06:57.000 --> 01:06:57.800
Yes, he did.

01:06:57.860 --> 01:06:59.880
He said it sounded like an AI algorithm.

01:06:59.880 --> 01:07:01.580
Oh, he did. I'm sorry.

01:07:01.580 --> 01:07:15.140
And I was going to say that the one thing that, you know, AI algorithms cannot figure out is where or whether or when an NES game belongs on a little thing we call the Essential Games List.

01:07:17.680 --> 01:07:23.780
Joe, The Adventures of Bayou Billy, the Essential Games List, is it on there?

01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:31.040
So I will say that I enjoyed this game a lot, as you guys could tell.

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:37.920
And I did consider voting this Essential.

01:07:39.060 --> 01:07:48.760
The only thing that prevented me was the main beat-em-up stage, which is probably more than a third of the game.

01:07:49.120 --> 01:07:51.360
What's that? Only most of it.

01:07:51.800 --> 01:07:52.940
Yeah, exactly. Exactly.

01:07:52.940 --> 01:07:58.060
Like, that was the one thing that I couldn't bring my—if that part was better, I would have voted this Essential.

01:07:58.460 --> 01:08:10.280
Because I think, first of all, yes, I keep saying it's a jack-of-all-trades where you might say, like, well, if it's not, like, great at anything, it's just good at everything, that wouldn't even be good enough either.

01:08:10.320 --> 01:08:12.440
But I think that there's value to that.

01:08:12.660 --> 01:08:17.740
I mean, there's a game—it's a little different because its hook is being co-op, but a way out.

01:08:17.860 --> 01:08:18.940
I don't know if anyone's played that.

01:08:18.940 --> 01:08:26.000
But, yeah, the hook is being co-op, but, like, forgetting about that, like, everything in that game is mediocre, like, gameplay.

01:08:26.020 --> 01:08:28.560
Yeah, but the sum is greater than the whole idea.

01:08:28.980 --> 01:08:31.980
Exactly. And that's kind of what I was getting out of this.

01:08:31.980 --> 01:08:36.680
Plus the fact that it has the practice mode, which, as you know, I love.

01:08:36.940 --> 01:08:49.980
And the tiny little cherry on top of the sound mode, which is just like, wow, these people really, really, like, cared and made this, like, overall solid game.

01:08:49.980 --> 01:09:07.540
But I just can't put a game on the essential list when one of the three gameplay level types that is by far the—encompasses the most of the game just is frustrating and somehow not very good.

01:09:07.620 --> 01:09:11.180
So I did end up saying no, but I'll call it a play it for sure.

01:09:11.500 --> 01:09:12.540
All right. Fair enough.

01:09:12.940 --> 01:09:48.520
Sean? And then the beat-em-up is, like, a D-minus.

01:09:48.940 --> 01:10:08.860
So even in, like, an average thing there, the fact that the majority of the game is so frustrating and the minigames—I would even call them minigames that you play between those levels aren't even that much better.

01:10:09.400 --> 01:10:12.340
This isn't going to sniff the essential games list for me.

01:10:12.340 --> 01:10:13.620
Anyway, well said, Sean.

01:10:13.940 --> 01:10:18.680
And the last thing, you know, when it comes down to how to do an essential games list is that I must vote.

01:10:18.940 --> 01:10:21.320
That is always the last thing that we must do.

01:10:21.500 --> 01:10:27.160
Even if I go first, we always have to make sure that it's okay with me at the end of the essential games list because I always speak last.

01:10:27.400 --> 01:10:29.420
So I will speak last here, and I will vote.

01:10:29.560 --> 01:10:34.580
And I am delaying because I'm not quite sure what to make of The Avengers of Bayou Billy.

01:10:34.680 --> 01:10:37.220
It's a flawed game, no doubt.

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:40.680
And it's not going on my essential games list, so I guess I could get that out of the way.

01:10:40.680 --> 01:10:42.920
But I don't know what's my final verdict here.

01:10:43.060 --> 01:10:46.220
It's Konami, and they usually don't let us down.

01:10:47.260 --> 01:10:50.840
But I don't know if it's because it's Konami that I'm so let down.

01:10:51.140 --> 01:10:59.720
Like, would I let, like, you know, another random publisher or developer who's just made, like, one or two games on the system, like, would I care as much?

01:11:00.800 --> 01:11:11.780
Adventures of Bayou Billy, like, had a chance to be a great beat-em-up game with, like, yeah, as Sean mentioned there, it's like, yeah, the other two are just, like, mini games, things that happen in the game.

01:11:11.860 --> 01:11:12.740
But it's a beat-em-up, right?

01:11:12.920 --> 01:11:18.100
But they missed the mark on the beat-em-up thing, and I'm not sure how that happened to them.

01:11:18.640 --> 01:11:29.400
I feel like, you know, Konami, Capcom, Nintendo, you know, these guys, like, know how to make games better than anyone else in 1988, you know, 1989 now.

01:11:29.600 --> 01:11:39.920
But, like, they know how to make these games, and they just completely missed the mark on, like, what made Double Dragon and even Renegade, you know, the first beat-em-up we played on the NES.

01:11:39.920 --> 01:11:43.340
Like, that was so much more fun than what we got here.

01:11:44.120 --> 01:11:46.240
But there was a lot of good stuff to say.

01:11:46.340 --> 01:11:49.860
We went on for a long time talking about this game, so there was a lot of good stuff.

01:11:50.120 --> 01:11:55.500
It's just, it's so flawed as an experience that I couldn't recommend this to anybody.

01:11:56.740 --> 01:12:07.820
I'm sorry to say that, you know, but, like, I think you've heard from me, you've heard from Sean, you've heard from Joe, and I think, you know, whoever you landed on the side of,

01:12:07.820 --> 01:12:21.660
the one thing you should take away from this is that just because you add in, you know, more types of gameplay or more variety to the gameplay, you know, doesn't mean you should.

01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:28.280
Maybe if they spent more time on the thinking about how the beat-em-up portion should work and making a game that was just a beat-em-up game,

01:12:28.480 --> 01:12:31.900
they would have came away with something more Konami-like and more solid.

01:12:31.900 --> 01:12:37.660
Like, imagine a Castlevania game that's not a platformer, but instead it's a beat-em-up.

01:12:37.760 --> 01:12:43.380
It's like, we could have got that game here, but instead they went for something that was more like a smorgasbord of a game.

01:12:44.200 --> 01:12:47.260
Mmm. And that sounds delicious.

01:12:47.440 --> 01:12:48.540
Yeah, that sounds delicious.

01:12:48.640 --> 01:12:50.360
Yeah, let's get some gumbo.

01:12:50.860 --> 01:12:51.860
Let's get some gumbo. Seafood gumbo.

01:12:52.020 --> 01:12:54.460
Yeah, some broils. Yeah, get some grits.

01:12:54.460 --> 01:12:56.420
All right. Some grits, yeah.

01:12:56.560 --> 01:12:57.680
What else did we eat down there?

01:12:59.060 --> 01:13:03.060
Yeah, we should mention that we went down to New Orleans for this episode to research.

01:13:03.060 --> 01:13:04.340
Yeah, this is live from New Orleans.

01:13:05.280 --> 01:13:08.780
Just to check it out and see what the deal was.

01:13:09.460 --> 01:13:17.860
No, in all seriousness, as most of the listener fan base knew, we didn't post last week because I wasn't feeling too well.

01:13:18.140 --> 01:13:22.480
And it means a lot to me that a lot of the fans reached out on Twitter and hoped that I would get better.

01:13:22.600 --> 01:13:24.500
And I did. You're all very good people.

01:13:25.240 --> 01:13:27.080
Yeah, you're all very good people, but we did it again.

01:13:27.080 --> 01:13:29.640
We recorded and we are delivering the episode this Friday.

01:13:29.780 --> 01:13:33.120
And you're listening to it right now on this Friday or a later Friday.

01:13:33.220 --> 01:13:36.600
You should just tweet at us and say which Friday you listen to it.

01:13:36.680 --> 01:13:40.000
But not like if you listen to it on a Saturday, Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday.

01:13:40.240 --> 01:13:42.280
Yeah, those don't. Round to the nearest Friday.

01:13:42.400 --> 01:13:43.540
I just want to hear about Fridays.

01:13:43.820 --> 01:13:45.380
Yeah. Nearest Friday.

01:13:45.540 --> 01:13:46.580
That's good, Joe. Yeah, exactly.

01:13:47.100 --> 01:13:50.400
So, like, the question there, Joe, do you round up or do you round down?

01:13:50.940 --> 01:13:55.900
Well, if you're exactly halfway there, you round up.

01:13:56.420 --> 01:13:58.440
Otherwise, you just decide which one you're closer to.

01:13:59.020 --> 01:14:03.240
So, if you listen to this thing on a Tuesday, you're going to let those people go back to Friday, the previous Friday.

01:14:03.240 --> 01:14:04.300
You're going to let them get that credit?

01:14:04.560 --> 01:14:06.400
Yeah, why not? Okay.

01:14:06.680 --> 01:14:08.300
I don't think. I guess it's really there's no stakes.

01:14:08.320 --> 01:14:12.140
I say give it to them. Right? There's no, like, no risks to be taken here.

01:14:12.400 --> 01:14:17.260
And you know what? Another thing you can do is if you listen to two on one Friday, you can bank that for another Friday.

01:14:17.640 --> 01:14:20.940
I think this episode just needs to fade out, like, while we're talking.

01:14:21.820 --> 01:14:22.940
Yeah, that's a good idea.

